[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider whether the problem might be a "gotcha." The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mGaussian curvature is defined for 2-dimensional manifolds (surfaces). The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproblem mentions R^n generally but asks about "Gaussian curvature." For n > 2, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwe'd talk about sectional curvature or Riemann curvature tensor. But the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquestion is essentially about whether curvature vanishes. I'll address this: for[0m[38;2;124;124;124mn = 2, Gaussian curvature vanishes; for general n, the Riemann curvature tensor [0m[38;2;124;124;124mvanishes (hence all sectional curvatures, including Gaussian curvature when n = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m2).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a clean proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet M be a Riemannian manifold with metric g. The hypothesis: for every point p [0m[38;2;124;124;124m∈ M, there exists an open neighborhood U of p and a coordinate chart φ: U → R^n [0m[38;2;124;124;124msuch that φ is an isometry onto its image, i.e., for every q ∈ U and all tangent[0m[38;2;124;124;124mvectors X, Y ∈ T_q M,[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mg_q(X, Y) = ⟨dφ_q(X), dφ_q(Y)⟩_Eucl.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn the coordinates (x^1, ..., x^n) given by φ, the coordinate vector fields ∂/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m∂x^i satisfy:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mg_ij(q) = g(∂/∂x^i, ∂/∂x^j)(q) = ⟨dφ(∂/∂x^i), dφ(∂/∂x^j)⟩ = ⟨e_i, e_j⟩ = δ_ij,[0m
[38;2;124;124;124msince dφ(∂/∂x^i) = e_i (the standard basis vector, because φ is the identity in [0m[38;2;124;124;124mits own coordinates — φ maps q to its coordinate tuple, so dφ(∂/∂x^i) = e_i).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore g_ij = δ_ij at every point of U. The metric components are constant on[0m[38;2;124;124;124mU.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Christoffel symbols are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mΓ^k_ij = (1/2) g^{kl} (∂_i g_jl + ∂_j g_il − ∂_l g_ij).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSince g_ij = δ_ij is constant, all partial derivatives ∂_l g_ij = 0, so Γ^k_ij =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m0 on U.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Riemann curvature tensor components:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mR^l_ijk = ∂_j Γ^l_ik − ∂_k Γ^l_ij + Γ^l_jm Γ^m_ik − Γ^l_km Γ^m_ij.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mAll Christoffel symbols and their derivatives vanish, so R^l_ijk = 0 on U.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince every point p ∈ M lies in such a neighborhood U, the Riemann curvature [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtensor vanishes everywhere on M. In particular, for n = 2, the Gaussian [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurvature K = R_1212 / det(g) = 0. For general n, all sectional curvatures [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvanish.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore M is locally flat. The answer is yes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m### PROOF COMPLETE[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me double check the reasoning about dφ(∂/∂x^i) = e_i. The chart φ: U → R^n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmaps a point q to (x^1(q), ..., x^n(q)). The coordinate vector field ∂/∂x^i is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdefined by dφ(∂/∂x^i) = e_i. That's the definition of coordinate vector fields. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo yes, this is correct. And the isometry condition gives g(∂/∂x^i, ∂/∂x^j) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m⟨e_i, e_j⟩ = δ_ij. Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is correct and complete.[0m

